\\mathrm{d}u=\\tan{\\left(u \\right)}+C$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=4 x + 3$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int 4 \\sec^{2}{\\left(4 x + 3 \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n\\int \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\tan{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\tan{\\left(4 x + 3 \\right)}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678192 :question/problem "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{8}}^{0} 4 \\sec^{2}{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\tan{\\left(u \\right)}+C$.\n\nEndpoint values: $\\tan{\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}=-1,\\quad \\tan{\\left(0 \\right)}=0$.\n\n{{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=- \\frac{\\pi}{8}&\\Longrightarrow u=- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\\\x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{8}}^{0} 4 \\sec^{2}{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=2\\left(\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0} \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\left[2 \\tan{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(-2\\right).\n$$\n\nThe answer is\n\n$$\n2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678200 :question/problem "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{8}} 4 \\csc^{2}{\\left(- 2 x + \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\csc^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=- \\cot{\\left(u \\right)}+C$.\n\nEndpoint values: $\\cot{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}=0,\\quad \\cot{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}=1$.\n\n{{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=- 2 x + \\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\\\x=\\frac{\\pi}{8}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new bounds decrease. Keep this order when evaluating the antiderivative.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{8}} 4 \\csc^{2}{\\left(- 2 x + \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=-2\\left(\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\csc^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\left[2 \\cot{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}=\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right).\n$$\n\nThe answer is\n\n$$\n2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678211 :question/problem "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{8}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} 3 \\csc^{2}{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\csc^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=- \\cot{\\left(u \\right)}+C$.\n\nEndpoint values: $\\cot{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}=1,\\quad \\cot{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}=0$.\n\n{{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\pi}{8}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\\\x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{8}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} 3 \\csc^{2}{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\csc^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\left[- \\frac{3 \\cot{\\left(u \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe answer is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678214 :question/problem "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int 3 \\sec^{2}{\\left(3 - 3 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\tan{\\left(u \\right)}+C$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3 - 3 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int 3 \\sec^{2}{\\left(3 - 3 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=-\\left(\\int \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n-\\left(\\int \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=- \\tan{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n- \\tan{\\left(3 - 3 x \\right)}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678216 :question/problem "Use the substitution $u=3 x$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\tan{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\int \\tan{\\left(u \\right)} \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\int \\tan{\\left(u \\right)} \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678217 :question/problem "Use the substitution $u=4 x$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int \\cot{\\left(4 x \\right)} \\csc{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=4 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\cot{\\left(4 x \\right)} \\csc{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\int \\cot{\\left(u \\right)} \\csc{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\int \\cot{\\left(u \\right)} \\csc{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678219 :question/problem "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 2 \\tan{\\left(- 2 x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(- 2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\tan{\\left(u \\right)} \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\sec{\\left(u \\right)}+C$.\n\nEndpoint values: $\\sec{\\left(0 \\right)}=1,\\quad \\sec{\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right)}=2$.\n\n{{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=- 2 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=0\\\\x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}&\\Longrightarrow u=- \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new bounds decrease. Keep this order when evaluating the antiderivative.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 2 \\tan{\\left(- 2 x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(- 2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=-\\left(\\int_{0}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\tan{\\left(u \\right)} \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\left[- \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{3}}=\\left(-2\\right)-\\left(-1\\right).\n$$\n\nThe answer is\n\n$$\n-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678233 :question/problem "Use the substitution $u=2 x$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int 2 \\csc^{2}{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int 2 \\csc^{2}{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int \\csc^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\int \\csc^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678237 :question/problem "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\cot{\\left(- 2 x + \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)} \\csc{\\left(- 2 x + \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\cot{\\left(u \\right)} \\csc{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=- \\csc{\\left(u \\right)}+C$.\n\nEndpoint values: $\\csc{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}=1,\\quad \\csc{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}=2$.\n\n{{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=- 2 x + \\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\\\x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new bounds decrease. Keep this order when evaluating the antiderivative.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\cot{\\left(- 2 x + \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)} \\csc{\\left(- 2 x + \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=- \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\cot{\\left(u \\right)} \\csc{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\frac{\\csc{\\left(u \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}=\\left(1\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe answer is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068918 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068570 :question/math-academy-id "e-467" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/4} \\dfrac{\\tan x \\sec^2 x}{1+\\tan^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u = 1+\\tan^2 x.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi/4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069003 :question/math-academy-id "q-10046" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\frac{\\sec x\\tan x}{1 + \\sec x}dx.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069378 :question/math-academy-id "q-49557" :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{2\\cos y}{\\sin y}dy\n$$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237790 :question/math-academy-id "q-10045" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)$ | $1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)}^{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)}^{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250148 :question/math-academy-id "q-113998" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2}\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320749 :question/math-academy-id "q-350887" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)+3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)+3}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320750 :question/math-academy-id "q-350901" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){cos}^{2}⁡(x)}{8{cos}^{3}⁡(x)+5}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=8{cos}^{3}⁡(x)+5,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{cos}^{3}⁡(x)+5)=-24{cos}^{2}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nSo, differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-24{cos}^{2}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){cos}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /3$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $6$ | $5$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){cos}^{2}⁡(x)}{8{cos}^{3}⁡(x)+5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{8{cos}^{3}⁡(x)+5}\\cdot 24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){cos}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{5}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{6}^{5}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{6}^{5} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320751 :question/math-academy-id "q-350904" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}{2+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=2+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}{2+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)}\\cdot (\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{4}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{4}^{3} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342058 :question/math-academy-id "q-73674" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}))=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSo, let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}).$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})}\\cdot (y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429930 :question/math-academy-id "q-113997" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={sec}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u={sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}(u-1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int (1-\\frac{1}{u})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =u-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=C+1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487529 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{12 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{12 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x &= 12\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= 12\\ln|u|+C \\\\ &= 12\\ln|\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487530 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{10 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x &= 10\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= 10\\ln|u|+C \\\\ &= 10\\ln|\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487536 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x &= 4\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= 4\\ln|u|+C \\\\ &= 4\\ln|\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487541 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{6 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x &= 6\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= 6\\ln|u|+C \\\\ &= 6\\ln|\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487549 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{8 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x &= 8\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= 8\\ln|u|+C \\\\ &= 8\\ln|\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580897 :question/math-academy-id "q-350908" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x)}{2+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=2+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x)}{2+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580904 :question/math-academy-id "q-350892" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}{2+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=2+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}{2+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{2+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580917 :question/math-academy-id "q-113987" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=1+{tan}^{3}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{tan}^{3}⁡x}\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+{tan}^{3}⁡x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+{tan}^{3}⁡x|}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580921 :question/math-academy-id "q-114003" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({cos}^{3}⁡x+1)=-3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u={cos}^{3}⁡x+1.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $9/8$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{9/8}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{9/8}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{2}^{9/8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{16}{9}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601906 :question/math-academy-id "q-114004" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }/2}\\frac{x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))=2x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSo, let $u=1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{\\pi }/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }/2}\\frac{x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }/2}\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})}\\cdot x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186605890 :question/math-academy-id "q-73679" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡z{csc}^{2}⁡z}{5+{cot}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(5+{cot}^{2}⁡z)=-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡z{csc}^{2}⁡z.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u=5+{cot}^{2}⁡z.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡z{csc}^{2}⁡z\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cot}⁡z{csc}^{2}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡z{csc}^{2}⁡z}{5+{cot}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\frac{1}{5+{cot}^{2}⁡z}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cot}⁡z{csc}^{2}⁡z)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\,(-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5+{cot}^{2}⁡z|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5+{cot}^{2}⁡z)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we used that fact that $5+{cot}^{2}⁡z>0$ for all $z.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611592 :question/math-academy-id "q-113992" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}(u-2)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int (1-\\frac{2}{u})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =u-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=C+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611635 :question/math-academy-id "q-350899" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡(x)}{2-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=2-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={csc}^{2}⁡(x)\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u={csc}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /4$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2-1/\\sqrt{3}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡(x)}{2-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{2-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}\\cdot {csc}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\,2-1/\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{\\,2-1/\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =ln\\,(2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186658911 :question/math-academy-id "q-350900" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({sin}^{2}⁡(x)+2)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nSo, differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $9/4$ | $11/4$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)+2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)+2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{9/4}^{11/4}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{9/4}^{11/4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{9/4}^{11/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{11}{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{11}{9}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186658945 :question/math-academy-id "q-346380" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡(x)+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u={cos}^{2}⁡(x)+2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡(x)+2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡(x)+2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{3}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677532 :question/problem "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{- \\sin{\\left(2 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(2 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{field-1}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\cos{\\left(2 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}.\n$$\n\nThe negative derivative is included in the factor outside the new integral.\n\nSubstituting, including the required constant factor, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{- \\sin{\\left(2 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(2 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\ln\\left|\\cos{\\left(2 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677534 :question/problem "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{8}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\frac{- 7 \\tan{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{4 \\sec{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=4 \\sec{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 3$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=8 \\tan{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\pi}{8}&\\Longrightarrow u=3 + 4 \\sqrt{2}\\\\x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}&\\Longrightarrow u=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting, including the required constant factor, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{8}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\frac{- 7 \\tan{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{4 \\sec{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=- \\frac{7}{8}\\left(\\int_{3 + 4 \\sqrt{2}}^{11} \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth new bounds are positive, so absolute values may be omitted. Evaluate:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{7}{8}\\left(\\left[\\ln u\\right]_{3 + 4 \\sqrt{2}}^{11}\\right)=- \\frac{7}{8}\\left(\\ln\\left(11\\right)-\\ln\\left(3 + 4 \\sqrt{2}\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nUsing $\\ln A-\\ln B=\\ln(A/B)$ gives\n\n$$\n- \\frac{7}{8}\\left(\\ln\\left(\\frac{11}{3 + 4 \\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677538 :question/problem "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{- 9 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{field-1}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 4$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=16 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, including the required constant factor, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{- 9 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=- \\frac{9}{16}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{9}{16}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=- \\frac{9}{16}\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{9}{16}\\left(\\ln\\left|4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 4\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677541 :question/problem "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{10}} \\frac{- 5 \\sin{\\left(5 x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(5 x \\right)} + 5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(5 x \\right)} + 5$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=40 \\sin{\\left(5 x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(5 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=5\\\\x=\\frac{\\pi}{10}&\\Longrightarrow u=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting, including the required constant factor, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{10}} \\frac{- 5 \\sin{\\left(5 x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(5 x \\right)} + 5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=- \\frac{1}{8}\\left(\\int_{5}^{9} \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth new bounds are positive, so absolute values may be omitted. Evaluate:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{1}{8}\\left(\\left[\\ln u\\right]_{5}^{9}\\right)=- \\frac{1}{8}\\left(\\ln\\left(9\\right)-\\ln\\left(5\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nUsing $\\ln A-\\ln B=\\ln(A/B)$ gives\n\n$$\n- \\frac{1}{8}\\left(\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right)\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677542 :question/problem "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{7 \\cot{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\csc{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{\\csc{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{field-1}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\csc{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 4$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- 2 \\cot{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\csc{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nThe negative derivative is included in the factor outside the new integral.\n\nSubstituting, including the required constant factor, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{7 \\cot{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\csc{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{\\csc{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=- \\frac{7}{2}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{7}{2}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=- \\frac{7}{2}\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{7}{2}\\left(\\ln\\left|\\csc{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 4\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677546 :question/problem "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{9 \\cos{\\left(4 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(4 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{field-1}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\sin{\\left(4 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4 \\cos{\\left(4 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, including the required constant factor, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{9 \\cos{\\left(4 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(4 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{9}{4}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=\\frac{9}{4}\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}\\left(\\ln\\left|\\sin{\\left(4 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677547 :question/problem "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{- \\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} \\sec^{2}{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{\\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{field-1}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4 \\sec^{2}{\\left(4 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)}=u-2$, the remaining quotient is\n\n$$\n\\frac{u-2}{u}=1-\\frac{2}{u}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, including the required constant factor, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{- \\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} \\sec^{2}{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{\\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=- \\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\int 1 - \\frac{2}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\int 1 - \\frac{2}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=- \\frac{1}{4}\\left(u-2\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2-2\\left(\\ln\\left|\\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2\\right|\\right)\\right)+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677553 :question/problem "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{9 \\sin{\\left(5 x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(5 x \\right)} + 3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{field-1}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(5 x \\right)} + 3$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- 30 \\sin{\\left(5 x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(5 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nThe negative derivative is included in the factor outside the new integral.\n\nSubstituting, including the required constant factor, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{9 \\sin{\\left(5 x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(5 x \\right)} + 3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=- \\frac{3}{10}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{3}{10}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=- \\frac{3}{10}\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{3}{10}\\left(\\ln\\left|3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(5 x \\right)} + 3\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677558 :question/problem "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{- 7 \\tan{\\left(5 x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{2 \\sec{\\left(5 x \\right)} + 5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{field-1}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2 \\sec{\\left(5 x \\right)} + 5$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=10 \\tan{\\left(5 x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(5 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, including the required constant factor, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{- 7 \\tan{\\left(5 x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{2 \\sec{\\left(5 x \\right)} + 5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=- \\frac{7}{10}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{7}{10}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=- \\frac{7}{10}\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{7}{10}\\left(\\ln\\left|2 \\sec{\\left(5 x \\right)} + 5\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677561 :question/problem "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{9 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{field-1}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, including the required constant factor, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{9 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{9}{4}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=\\frac{9}{4}\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}\\left(\\ln\\left|\\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\right|\\right)+C.\n