ac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2 \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{2 \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(x \\right)}}{2 \\sec{\\left(x \\right)} + 3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678424 :question/problem "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{2 \\csc^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\cot{\\left(x \\right)} + 3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int 1/u\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\ln\\left|u\\right|+C$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\cot{\\left(x \\right)} + 3$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- \\csc^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{2 \\csc^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\cot{\\left(x \\right)} + 3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=-2\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n-2\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=-2\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n-2\\left(\\ln\\left|\\cot{\\left(x \\right)} + 3\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678427 :question/problem "Use the substitution $u=2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 2$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 2$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=- \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n- \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678436 :question/problem "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{x \\cos{\\left(x^{2} \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x^{2} \\right)} + 3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int 1/u\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\ln\\left|u\\right|+C$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\sin{\\left(x^{2} \\right)} + 3$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2 x \\cos{\\left(x^{2} \\right)}.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{x \\cos{\\left(x^{2} \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x^{2} \\right)} + 3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\ln\\left|\\sin{\\left(x^{2} \\right)} + 3\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678437 :question/problem "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int 1/u\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\ln\\left|u\\right|+C$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\sin^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\ln\\left|\\sin^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678441 :question/problem "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\cos^{3}{\\left(x \\right)} + 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int 1/u\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\ln\\left|u\\right|+C$.\n\nEndpoint values: $\\cos{\\left(0 \\right)}=1,\\quad \\cos{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}=0$.\n\n{{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\cos^{3}{\\left(x \\right)} + 2$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- 3 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=3\\\\x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new bounds decrease. Keep this order when evaluating the antiderivative.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\cos^{3}{\\left(x \\right)} + 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=-\\left(\\int_{3}^{2} \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth transformed bounds are positive. Evaluating gives\n\n$$\n-\\left(\\left[\\ln u\\right]_{3}^{2}\\right)=-\\left(\\ln\\left(2\\right)-\\ln\\left(3\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nThe answer is\n\n$$\n-\\left(\\ln\\left(2\\right)-\\ln\\left(3\\right)\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678444 :question/problem "Use the substitution $u=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\sec^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\sec^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{\\sec^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678446 :question/problem "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} \\sec^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sec{\\left(x \\right)} + 3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int 1/u\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\ln\\left|u\\right|+C$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\sec{\\left(x \\right)} + 3$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\sec{\\left(x \\right)}=u-3$, rewrite the remaining factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{u-3}{u}=1-\\frac{3}{u}.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} \\sec^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sec{\\left(x \\right)} + 3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int 1 - \\frac{3}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n\\int 1 - \\frac{3}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=u-3\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\sec{\\left(x \\right)} + 3-3\\left(\\ln\\left|\\sec{\\left(x \\right)} + 3\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}]}
